iMac 5K dual-tile panel: fix the two boot-time display artifacts.

Applies on top of imac5k-amdgpu-7.2.2.patch.

1. Skewed Apple logo on a WARM reboot
   The stitch paths wake the slave tile by writing the vendor panel-latch
   DPCD (0x4F1 = 1) when the slave stream is enabled, and never clear it.
   The woken state therefore survives a warm reboot, and Apple's firmware,
   which assumes the factory single-link state, draws its boot logo into a
   dual-tile panel and skews it. A cold boot power-cycles the panel, which
   is why it only shows up on warm reboots.

   Fix: mirror the enable path. dp_write_tiled_stream_disable_latch() writes
   0x4F1 = 0 on the root and slave links from link_set_dpms_off(), after
   blank_stream() (nothing on screen to skew) and before disable_link()
   (AUX still up). Every modeset then leaves the panel exactly as a cold
   boot found it and the existing wake/train/enable-latch sequence brings
   it back. On reboot, the display teardown in the suspend path runs this
   naturally, so the firmware gets a single-link panel.

   Clearing alone is not enough: measured over the DP AUX chardev, the
   clear takes effect instantly (root and slave both read back 0) but the
   panel raises HPD-RX and the detect path wakes the tile straight back up
   within 2-10 ms. At reboot the stream-off runs before IRQs are suspended,
   so that same interrupt wins the race. dc.apple_5k_going_down, set from
   amdgpu_pci_shutdown() before the display is torn down, turns the wake
   paths (root latch pulse, slave stream-enable latch) into no-ops, so the
   clear done by the teardown is the last word the panel hears.

   An earlier version cleared the latch from amdgpu_pci_shutdown() itself.
   That ran while the shutdown splash was still being scanned out, so the
   splash skewed, and it did not fix the firmware logo. Removed.

2. Half-dark panel at the disk-encryption password prompt
   The slave tile only gets a DC stream during an atomic modeset (see the
   stitch block in amdgpu_dm_atomic_check), and drm_client_setup()'s
   initial fbdev config deliberately stops short of committing one: it
   probes, sets up the crtcs and calls register_framebuffer(), leaving the
   commit to a later hotplug or to fbcon taking over the console. With
   "quiet splash" fbcon defers take-over, so on this machine that first
   commit landed 1.16 s after fb0 went live (5.911 s -> 7.069 s in the boot
   log). For that whole window the panel presents the full-width stitched
   mode while only the root tile scans out, and the right-hand half is
   dark -- long enough to cover the password prompt.

   Fix: once fbdev is set up, if this device drives a stitched tile panel,
   issue a second client hotplug. That takes the dev->fb_helper path, which
   does commit, so the peer tile stream is created during probe instead of
   whenever something else happens to trigger a modeset. Confirmed on
   hardware: peer stream added 3 ms after fb0, down from 1160 ms.

Both changes are no-ops on hardware without the tiled-panel quirk.

--- a/drivers/gpu/drm/amd/amdgpu/amdgpu.h
